- **The compiled checker reads a `comprises` clause as the 2012 texts do for a generic subtrait: it may extend the closed trait when the trait table knows at least one type that immediately extends it and each such type is below a listed type** (`compile-ladder/rung-comprises-checker/`; POSITIONS, "`AnyIntegral`'s closure and how the checker reads `comprises`"; rows 459, 487-490). `isEligibleToExtend`'s third disjunct, `everyKnownSubtypeListed` (`scala_src/typechecker/TypeHierarchyChecker.scala:247-287`), over the trait table's known types compared by simple name (`TraitTable.scala:99-110`). `AnyIntegral`'s clause (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:433`) checks as written, so the `FortressLibrary` api reaches its overloading and return-type checks on the count stage, 5 errors at the last landed gate (`compile-ladder/climb-batch-8/gate/checker-count.txt`; "The true distance to the switch-over" above). Gated by `compiler_tests/ComprisesGenericSubtrait.test`, with `XXX10i`, `XXX9z`, `XXXComprisesGenericUnlisted.test` and `XXXComprisesGenericRenamed.test` refused; the ellipsis refusal (row 354) is untouched. Open: another unit's extender (row 487), the distance stage's moves (row 488), the extender's spelling (row 489) and the simple-name match (row 490), parked for Pavol with `checkDeclComprises`'s header comment (`TypeHierarchyChecker.scala:143-147`; `coordinator/PLAN.md`, "Off the path, parked").

== explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md, ## The specification and the repository's lineage

FACTS.md:158
- **The specification reads a `comprises` clause as the later Types chapter does: every value of the trait is a value of a listed type, and a trait with static parameters may stand unlisted between** (`compile-ladder/rung-spec-comprises/decision-record.md`). Rendered normative text replaces the draft note (`Specification/basic/traits.tex:235-252`): a listed type may be an instantiation over `T`'s static parameters only, so `comprises Integral[\I\] where [\I\]` is no valid clause, and an explicit extender is below a listed type, a closed trait meeting the same requirement, or a trait with static parameters every extender of which is below a listed type. Its callout (`:253-267`) and Appendix I's I.1.20 (`Specification/appendices/changes.tex:1257-1343`) quote the note; the ellipsis sentence stays in its note (`traits.tex:268-274`); a listed bare type variable is open (row 407); the checker's departures are rows 487, 489 and 490. The four passages that read a clause at the level of types read it at the level of values (`Specification/basic/types-vals-vars.tex:583-627`, `Specification/advanced/overloading.tex:339-372`, `Specification/basic/functions.tex:370-421`, `Specification/basic/components/source-code.tex:428-434`; row 491, fixed), and both paths run the Meet Rule's example (row 492), gated by `tests/ComprisesMeetWalk.fss` and `compiler_tests/ComprisesMeetCompiled.test`.

== explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md, ## The checker and the one library

FACTS.md:85
- **The compiled checker judges two functional methods by the Meet Rule for Functional Methods, per providing type, and its overloading memo no longer answers across contexts** (`compile-ladder/rung-overloading-checker/REPORT.md`; rows 556, 557, 477, 561, 562, 568 and 569 fixed). Two functional methods at one self position are valid among the top-level functionals; every type that provides both must provide the meet, except a pair one of whose declarations is the component's own abstract one, which no check reads (row 572): the check reads every declaration in an api, and in a component those with bodies and every one inherited from an api, and compares the meet without the self parameter wherever it sits. An inherited method's static parameters are renamed apart from its trait's; the memo is keyed on the check's inputs; an excludes clause's reciprocal entry is read with its trait's parameters in scope; a character literal is typed by each world's library. An object expression is checked by no per-provider rule, so a pair only it provides goes unchecked (row 570, home 2, behind row 375's code-generation stop). What the rule and the `Character` typing move in the count and the distance (top-level pairs and captured sites gone, pairs per providing type in the apis and the components, errors unmasked) is in REPORT section 10. Gated by `compiler_tests/FunctionalMethodMeetPerProvider`, `FunctionalMethodMeetSelfSecond`, `XXXFunctionalMethodMeetInheritedFromApi`, `GenericTraitExcludesKindEnv`, `InheritedMethodStaticParamSameName`, `InheritedFunctionalMethodStaticParamSameName` and `XXXFunctionalMethodMeetJoinOfClosedTraits`.

== explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md, ## The harness and the gate

FACTS.md:101
- **An `XXX` compile test pinned by `compile_err_contains` whose program compiles is reported as a wrong failure, not a missing one** (`compile-ladder/rung-unknown-size-arm/probes/pre-edit-tests.txt`). The harness evaluates the check key whether or not the command failed, so a missed key fails with "Saw wrong failure" even when the compile exited 0 (`tests/unit_tests/FileTests.java:384-402`); "Missing expected failure" appears only with no key or a met one. Either way the test is red. A compile that throws is checked on its `exception` key, " OK Saw expected exception" (`:341-361`).

FACTS.md:94
- **The `XXX` expected-failure mechanism in `compiler_tests/` and `library_tests/` can express a compile-stage failure only, and a run-time defect needs two `.test` files** (`compile-ladder/rung-tryatomic/`). `shouldFail` comes from the `.test` file name (`FileTests.java:932`) and reaches every stage the file drives (`:1052-1069`), so an `XXX` file that carries `link` or `compile` demands that the compile fail (`:384-404`), while an unsatisfied `run_out_*` verdict fails whatever `shouldFail` says (`:534-539`, `:583-585`; `pass`/`PASS` is the default check, `:276-282`). A compiles-but-crashes defect is therefore two `.test` files over one component, a plain-named one driving `link` and an `XXX`-named one driving `run` with a marker the failing run prints; a refusal is one compile test. Run in one JVM (`junit.sh`'s `ONE_JVM=1`, `compile-ladder/climb-batch-N/merged-tests/junit.sh`), the compile and link tests run before the run tests (`FileTests.java:997-1004`).

FACTS.md:96
- **`testSystem`'s four shards are one suite split by sorted index, so per-shard counts are not comparable across a change to the interpreter corpus and only their sum is** (`compile-ladder/climb-batch-2/JUDGE-gate.md`). `FileTests.java:790-806` sorts the files of `tests/` and keeps index `j` when `j % 4 == i` (root `build.xml:1171-1211`, the `systemShard` macro), so a file added moves every later entry along the shards (`compile-ladder/climb-batch-2/repair/shard-membership.sh`). `gate_compare` (the gate's step 5, in `gateRole` of `coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.js`) sums the `system-<i>` rows before comparing.

FACTS.md:92
- **`ant compileAll` deletes a tracked file** (reproduced at every gather). `compileAll` depends on `compileCommon` (`build.xml:539`), which depends on `cleanCache` (`:715`), which deletes `default_repository/caches` entire (`:42,356-359`) — including the tracked `global.map`, the linker's persisted `RepoState` (`linker/RepoState.java:176`). Every tree that runs `ant compileAll` therefore shows `D default_repository/caches/global.map` afterwards, and a `git add -A` at that moment commits the deletion; restore it with `git checkout -- default_repository/caches/global.map`.

